      "grounding": {
        "generic": [
          {
            "url": "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/business/2019/09/10/chinese-factories-struggle-african-swine-fever-sends-pig-prices/",
            "title": "Chinese factories struggle as African swine fever sends pig prices ...",
            "snippets": [
              "# Chinese factories struggle as African swine fever sends pig prices skyrocketing\nSubscribe\nSubscribe\n- email\n\npork\n\nPork prices surged in August",
              "Published 10 September 2019 1:42pm BST\n\nChinese factory prices tumbled at their fastest pace in three years in August, stoking hopes of more government action as demand for goods is squeezed by the trade war with the US.\n\nThe producer price index, which tracks factory prices, dropped deeper into deflation territory to sink 0.8pc year-on-year, down from a 0.3pc fall the previous month",
              "The accelerating decline indicated that the manufacturing industry is struggling to cope with the recent escalation in tariff tensions and spurred hopes of more stimulus from Beijing to jump-start the economy.\n\nFreya Beamish, economist at Pantheon Macro, argued the Chinese government would “not cease easing” until it sees “definitive signs” factory prices are starting to recover.\n\nThe consumer price index remained steady at 2.8pc despite a surge in pork prices as the industry is decimated by African swine fever",
              "Pork prices leapt 47pc year on year as China is forced to import more of the meat to make up for a huge shortfall caused by the disease. Food prices climbed 10pc.\n\nPig stock has declined by a third in China after the virus spread across Asia, causing prices to hit a record high. A cull of pigs to stop the fever, which is harmless to humans, had kept a lid on prices but they have started to soar in recent months",
              "Ms Beamish said the steady CPI reading “does not accurately reflect the pick-up in food prices”. She predicted that inflation would push through the central bank’s 3pc target early next year as the government attempts to address the price pressures through handouts.\n\n“As firms struggle with lower factory prices they face the additional pressure of increasing wage demands from workers as food prices increase,” warned Alastair Munro at commodities broker Marex Spectron",
              "However, he added: “Bad news such as this is largely expected and the financial markets appear hesitant about betting on further price declines amidst the call for further central bank stimulus measures.”\n\nBeijing has announced a slew of measures to stem the country's sharp slowdown as its factories fail to stage a recovery. Industrial production growth slipped to 4.8pc year-on-year in July, hitting a 17-year low in a surprise setback for the country’s factories"
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.japantimes.co.jp/news/2019/09/16/business/china-industrial-output-growth/",
            "title": "China's industrial output weaker than expected as pace of economic ...",
            "snippets": [
              "# China’s industrial output weaker than expected as pace of economic growth slows\nX Facebook LinkedIn Reddit Bluesky Threads Email Print Bookmark story Bookmark story Copy link\n\nSep 16, 2019\n\nBEIJING – The pace of growth in China’s economy slowed further in August, indicating that current stimulus policies may not be enough to shield the economy from the worsening effects of the trade war with the U.S",
              "Industrial output rose 4.4 percent from a year earlier, versus a median estimate of 5.2 percent. Retail sales expanded 7.5 percent, compared to a projected 7.9 percent increase. Fixed-asset investment slowed to 5.5 percent in the first eight months, versus a forecast 5.7 percent",
              "The industrial output reading was the lowest single-month figure since 2002, with only a combined Jan-Feb result in 2009 dipping lower. China merges some statistics due to the Lunar New Year holiday.\n\nThe data form further evidence that policy makers’ efforts to brake the slowdown in the economy are falling behind, as the nation faces structural downward forces at home and the likelihood of yet-higher tariffs on exports to the U.S"
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://edition.cnn.com/2019/09/16/economy/china-economy-industrial-production/",
            "title": "China’s economy is getting worse. That makes a trade deal more ...",
            "snippets": [
              "# China’s economy is getting worse. That makes a trade deal more likely\nChina’s economic slowdown keeps getting worse. That could give the country incentive to repair its trading relationship with the United States and take more steps to stimulate its economy.\n\nThe country released data Monday that showed industrial production — an important indicator for China’s economy — increased by just 4.4% in August compared to a year earlier. That’s worse than the sector’s performance in July, when it grew by 4.8%, its weakest growth in 17 years",
              "The world’s second biggest economy has been struggling because of its trade war with the United States. It’s also facing domestic challenges as it tries to rely less on debt to fuel growth.\nRelated article\n\nUS soybean farmers have been hit hard by the trade war with China.\n\nJOHANNES EISELE/AFP/Getty Images\n\nChina exempts US soybeans and pork from tariffs"
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.cnn.com/2019/09/16/economy/china-economy-industrial-production/index.html",
            "title": "China’s economy is getting worse. That makes a trade deal more ...",
            "snippets": [
              "# China’s economy is getting worse. That makes a trade deal more likely\nIndustrial production is important because it measures the output of key businesses in China’s manufacturing, mining and utilities sectors. The latest figure was also worse than the 5.2% growth that analysts polled by Reuters expected.\n\nOther data released Monday by China’s National Bureau of Statistics was also poor. Retail sales growth slowed to 7.5% in August, down from July’s 7.6% year-on-year uptick",
              "The soft August figures reflect an “increasing downside risk to the economy” as the trade war rages on, said Ken Cheung Kin Tai, chief foreign exchange strategist for Asia at Mizuho Bank in Hong Kong. “Against this backdrop, it makes sense that China softened its stance on trade talks” and introduced stimulus plans in recent weeks.\n\nThe weak data also fuels speculation about how China’s central bank will continue responding to the slowdown, Cheung added"
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.reuters.com/article/us-china-economy-activity/chinas-slowdown-deepens-industrial-output-growth-falls-to-17-1-2-year-low-idUSKBN1W102H/",
            "title": "China's slowdown deepens; industrial output growth falls to 17-1/2 ...",
            "snippets": [
              "# China's slowdown deepens; industrial output growth falls to 17-1/2 year low\nBEIJING (Reuters) - The slowdown in China's economy deepened in August, with growth in industrial production at its weakest 17-1/2 years amid spreading pain from a trade war with the United States and softening domestic demand.\n\nRetail sales and investment gauges worsened too, data released on Monday showed, reinforcing views that China is likely to cut some key interest rates this week for the first time in over three years to prevent a sharper slump in activity",
              "Industrial output growth unexpectedly weakened to 4.4% in August from the same period a year earlier, the slowest pace since February 2002 and receding from 4.8% in July. Analysts polled by Reuters had forecast a pick-up to 5.2%",
              "In particular, the value of delivered industrial exports fell 4.3% on-year, the first monthly decline since at least two years, Reuters records showed, reflecting the toll that the escalating Sino-U.S. trade war is taking on Chinese manufacturers.\n\nThe protracted trade war escalated dramatically last month, with President Donald Trump announcing new tariffs on Chinese goods from Sept. 1, and China letting its yuan currency sharply weaken days later",
              "Nomura's Lu expected September's industrial output to be further hampered by an anti-pollution campaign ahead of and during a key anniversary of the founding of the People's Republic of China on Oct.\n\nThe gloomy August activity data added to signs of broad-based economic weakness, following soft trade and credit reports last week",
              "Data out last week showed producer prices falling at their fastest pace in three years.\n\nThat followed a factory survey that showed activity shrank for the fourth straight month as the trade war wore on."
            ]
          }
        ],
        "map": []
